   "text": "I read this as a glycopeptide: a bicyclic-type thioether-cyclised peptide linked through a hydrazide/oxime linker to a branched, phosphorylated mannose pentasaccharide. This SMILES is built from my own reading of the drawing, not computed or checked by software.\n\n**Peptide.** The peptide is a Lys-NH2 whose side chain carries the linker. Its \u03b1-N is acylated by Gly, then Cys. The Cys is thioether-bridged (S\u2013CH2\u2013C(=O)) to the Phe at the start of the ring. Going round the ring from Phe, the residues are: Phe, Val, Pro, Thr, Thr, biphenylalanine (Bip), N-Me-Ala, Bip, N-Me-Ala, Glu, Ala, Pro, and back to Cys.\n\n**Peptide stereochemistry.** From the wedges and hashes I worked out L configuration at Lys, Cys, Phe, Val, both Pro, both Thr, both Bip, Glu and the three Ala. The lower Thr side chain is drawn with a hashed OH and comes out 3R. The other Thr side-chain carbon is drawn with a plain OH bond, so I left it unspecified.\n\n**Linker.** From the Lys \u03b5-NH the chain runs 4-C(=O)-benzoyl, CH=N\u2013O (drawn E, with aryl and O on opposite sides), O\u2013CH2\u2013C(=O)\u2013NH\u2013NH\u2013C(=O), then (CH2)3\u2013O to the reducing sugar.\n\n**Sugars.** I took all residues as D-mannopyranose and the glycosidic bond to the linker as \u03b2. All other glycosidic bonds are taken as \u03b1. The connectivity I read is:\n- Man-6-phosphate (\u03b11\u21922) Man (\u03b11\u21926) Man (\u03b11\u21926) Man, where the last Man is the \u03b2-linked reducing-end residue.\n- A second Man-6-phosphate (\u03b11\u21922) Man branch (\u03b11\u21923) on that reducing-end Man.\n\nSMILES: NC(=O)[C@H](CCCCNC(=O)c1ccc(cc1)/C=N/OCC(=O)NNC(=O)CCCO[C@@H]%10O[C@H](CO[C@H]%11O[C@H](CO[C@H]%12O[C@H](CO)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@@H]%12O[C@H]%13O[C@H](COP(=O)(O)O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@@H]%13O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@@H]%11O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O[C@H]%14O[C@H](CO)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@@H]%14O[C@H]%15O[C@H](COP(=O)(O)O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@@H]%15O)[C@@H]%10O)NC(=O)CNC(=O)[C@@H]2CSCC(=O)N[C@@H](Cc3